WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.259 --> 00:00:03.731
- There are only about 150 speakers

00:00:03.731 --> 00:00:05.989
of the Ocracoke brogue now,

00:00:05.989 --> 00:00:10.527
and they all live on one
end of this barrier island

00:00:10.527 --> 00:00:12.749
on the coast of North Carolina,

00:00:12.749 --> 00:00:16.646
which is basically an enlarged sand bar.

00:00:16.646 --> 00:00:20.724
Within the next 50 years,
the brogue will disappear.

00:00:20.724 --> 00:00:22.648
- Hoi toi to the sound
side so laugh like water

00:00:22.648 --> 00:00:23.481
far night moonshine no fish.

00:00:23.481 --> 00:00:26.731
(boat engine bubbling)

00:00:28.656 --> 00:00:31.761
- [Man] The dialect on this
island is sometimes referred to

00:00:31.761 --> 00:00:34.955
as hoi toiters which is their
pronunciation of high tide.

00:00:34.955 --> 00:00:38.538
- Hoi toid on the sound sides
high tide on the sound side.

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:41.828
- In the United States the
Ocracoke brogue is probably

00:00:41.828 --> 00:00:44.727
the only dialect that is not identified

00:00:44.727 --> 00:00:46.497
as being from America.

00:00:46.497 --> 00:00:48.607
- I do, I have a lot of people
that think I'm from Australia

00:00:48.607 --> 00:00:50.024
or Ireland, yeah.

00:00:51.178 --> 00:00:53.887
- [Man] You can look in
this boat and see crabs.

00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:55.247
- You know, the first people that came here

00:00:55.247 --> 00:00:58.216
from England and Ireland and
Scotland just stayed isolated.

00:00:58.216 --> 00:01:01.799
I'm from Ocracoke, I
am the 10th generation

00:01:03.218 --> 00:01:05.660
in my family from Ocracoke.

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:08.327
That goes back to the mid-1600s.

00:01:10.035 --> 00:01:12.866
- The reason this dialect was perpetuated

00:01:12.866 --> 00:01:15.948
for a couple of centuries
was because the people were

00:01:15.948 --> 00:01:17.316
very isolated.

00:01:17.316 --> 00:01:21.483
They didn't have much contact
with people from the mainland.

00:01:25.190 --> 00:01:27.693
Ocracoke is an island that has always

00:01:27.693 --> 00:01:29.298
lived around the water.

00:01:29.298 --> 00:01:30.628
- Got it!

00:01:30.628 --> 00:01:32.218
- Many of the unique items,

00:01:32.218 --> 00:01:34.799
particularly the vocabulary items,

00:01:34.799 --> 00:01:36.966
are built around the water

00:01:37.804 --> 00:01:40.647
and the sand and the weather.

00:01:40.647 --> 00:01:41.866
- The wind's blowin' really hard, he says,

00:01:41.866 --> 00:01:43.978
it's blowin harder then
a pop car right there.

00:01:43.978 --> 00:01:44.938
You know.

00:01:44.938 --> 00:01:48.098
- So you're takin' a
ride around the island.

00:01:48.098 --> 00:01:50.876
You're just taking a little scud.

00:01:50.876 --> 00:01:54.654
- When we come out here and
the wind's blowin' really hard

00:01:54.654 --> 00:01:57.516
and we take a bad beatin'.

00:01:57.516 --> 00:01:59.666
Then you could say we be a mommuck.

00:01:59.666 --> 00:02:03.244
- You also find here lots
of terms for outsiders.

00:02:03.244 --> 00:02:04.255
- Ya'll are dingbatters.

00:02:04.255 --> 00:02:06.793
(laughing)

00:02:06.793 --> 00:02:09.596
- My wife is from Maryland.

00:02:09.596 --> 00:02:12.084
We've been married 43 years.

00:02:12.084 --> 00:02:14.105
She's still a dingbatter.

00:02:14.105 --> 00:02:15.775
(laughing)

00:02:15.775 --> 00:02:17.404
- That's not a bad thing, OK?

00:02:17.404 --> 00:02:20.436
(laughing)

00:02:20.436 --> 00:02:23.386
- It was basically the dingbatters
who changed the dialect

00:02:23.386 --> 00:02:25.396
because so many people came in.

00:02:25.396 --> 00:02:29.693
Even today there are more
off-islanders who live here

00:02:29.693 --> 00:02:31.026
than on-landers.

00:02:31.975 --> 00:02:34.516
- Now it's changing, within
time we're all gonna lose it,

00:02:34.516 --> 00:02:35.495
ya' know?

00:02:35.495 --> 00:02:38.844
- Because of so many people coming in.

00:02:38.844 --> 00:02:43.013
It's a part of heritage that I'm proud of.

00:02:43.013 --> 00:02:44.223
- Some people think we talk funny.

00:02:44.223 --> 00:02:45.564
I think that's the way we talk.

00:02:45.564 --> 00:02:46.743
(laughs)

00:02:46.743 --> 00:02:48.255
It ain't funny to us,
but it doesn't matter,

00:02:48.255 --> 00:02:50.724
I mean, that's fine, you know?

00:02:50.724 --> 00:02:53.974
(plucked string music)

